<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yahya Muhammad Hamid ed-Din</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1869–1948</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Imam Yahya, Yahya bin Muhammad Hamid al-Din, Yahya Muhammad Hamid al-Din</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yahya Muhammad Hamid al-Din was the Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> from 1904 and king of the Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen from 1918 to 1948. He led the resistance to Ottoman rule, won autonomy by the Treaty of Daan in 1911, and took Sanaa when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> withdrew. He kept Yemen isolated, lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizan">Jizan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a> to Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in 1934, and was assassinated in a coup in 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din.jpg" alt="Yahya Muhammad Hamid ed-Din" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aamen Rihani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imam_yahya_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
